<h2>The prosecution case and the conviction</h2>
<p>The allegation was that in May 2023 the appellant enticed away a girl said to be a minor on the pretext of marriage, took her to Maharashtra for around twenty days, sexually abused her and made photographs and videos. Her brother lodged the written complaint on 5 July 2023 — forty-one days after the alleged abduction. The trial court convicted the appellant under Sections 376(3) and 363 IPC and Section 4 of the POCSO Act and imposed twenty years of rigorous imprisonment. The appeal, with an application for suspension of sentence, came before the Division Bench.</p>

<h2>Three soft points in the record</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Age.</strong> The victim's minority — indispensable both for Section 376(3) IPC (rape of a woman under sixteen) and for the POCSO conviction — was inadequately established: no original school admission records, and no ossification test to fall back on.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation.</strong> The prosecution's narrative of forcible abduction from school found no documented support in the investigating officers' evidence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Credibility.</strong> The Bench found the victim's account prima facie open to question, noting also issues around electronic evidence and Section 65B certification.</div>
</div>
<p>On these tentative findings — expressly stated to be for the limited purpose of the application — the Bench suspended the sentence and directed release on bail of ₹25,000 with two sureties of the like amount, pending the appeal.</p>

<h2>Suspension in long-sentence appeals: the balancing exercise</h2>
<p>The jurisprudence distinguishes between life sentences, where suspension is exceptional, and fixed-term sentences, where the court weighs arguable merit against gravity with a more open hand. A twenty-year term sits at the severe end of the fixed-term spectrum, and Division Benches do not suspend such sentences casually. What moves the needle is the nature of the arguable points: a challenge that goes to the foundational facts of the conviction — the age that makes the special statute applicable, the occurrence itself — is qualitatively different from a challenge to sentencing discretion. Where the former is genuinely arguable and the appeal will realistically take years, requiring the appellant to serve a substantial slice of a possibly unsustainable sentence raises exactly the concern Section 430 BNSS exists to address.</p>

<h2>The recurring lesson on age proof</h2>
<p>This is the second appellate order within days — across different High Courts — turning on defective proof of the victim's age, and the pattern deserves emphasis. Age is provable with modest diligence: summon the school's record custodian with the original admission register, obtain the birth certificate from the municipal registrar, and where documents genuinely do not exist, conduct the ossification test and prove it through the radiologist. Each step is routine; the failure to take any of them is what converts convictions into arguable appeals. Prosecuting agencies protect child victims most effectively not by charging under the special statute but by proving its threshold fact beyond challenge.</p>

<h2>Perspective</h2>
<p>Nothing in the order acquits the appellant, and nothing in this discussion doubts the gravity of offences against children. The appeal remains to be heard in full, with the statutory presumptions and the entire record in play. What the order illustrates — for appellate practice in Patna, Delhi and everywhere else — is the discipline of interim relief: tentative findings, confined purpose, conditions attached, and the final reckoning reserved for the hearing of the appeal itself.</p>
